  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 200 AM Monday... \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Much of the upcoming workweek will be unseasonably \nwarm and dry.\n\nBroad mid/upper ridging over the Southeastern US (in between \ntroughing over the North Atlantic that will absorb the remnants of \nIsaias and a closed low over the Intermountain West) will bring \nmostly sunny and dry weather to central NC for much of the upcoming \nworkweek. A weak shortwave and associated surface low will track \neast from the Great Lakes into northern New England from Tuesday \ninto early Wednesday, dropping a backdoor cold front southward into \nthe Mid-Atlantic. SW flow between the low to the north and high \npressure just offshore will bring increasing temperatures to start \nthe week, with highs in the upper-70s to lower-80s on Monday and \nlower-to-mid-80s on Tuesday. Guidance has continued to trend slower \nwith the backdoor front, and it is now unclear if it even makes it \nthis far south. This results in a very warm forecast from Wednesday \nthrough Friday, with highs in the mid-to-upper-80s and lows in the \nlower-to-mid-60s. Thursday has potential to be a little cooler with \nsome cloud cover in the north, but that will depend on how far south \nthe front is able to get. Any precipitation with the front will be \nminimal and largely pinned over VA due to the ridge in place.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... A strong but moisture-starved cold front will cross \nthe region late Friday, bringing a significant cooldown over the \nweekend with patchy frost not out of the question.\n\nA much more vigorous northern stream shortwave will dive down from \nCanada into the northern Plains and Great Lakes region on Thursday \nand Friday, potentially interacting with the closed low over the \nWestern US and deepening as it shifts into the Northeast US. The \nECMWF and GFS have come into better agreement on the timing and \noverall pattern evolution, dragging a strong cold front through the \narea late Friday. The best height falls will be well to our north so \nany associated precipitation would be light, and POPs are only \nslight chance at best. \n\nA significant cooldown to below-normal temperatures appears likely \nbehind the front over the weekend as Canadian high pressure builds \nin. Forecast highs on Saturday and Sunday are only in the lower-to-\nmid-60s, which is about 8-12 degrees below normal. Patchy frost may \neven be a concern, most likely on Saturday night as this is when low-\nlevel thicknesses bottom out. While most places have forecast lows \nin the upper-30s to lower-40s, isolated mid-30s aren't out of the \nquestion in cooler outlying areas according to some MOS guidance.",
